1. INTRODUCTION: WHY A2 COMES LAST IN THE CORE

1.1 The Dependency Chain

```
State (A4) + Predicates (A5) + FSM (A1) + Plans (A3)
  ↓
Actions (A2)
  ├── Consumed by VERIFY (A5)
  ├── Referenced by Plans (A3 §2.2)
  ├── Committed by FSM (A1 §4.2)
  └── Effects applied to State (A4 §5.2)
```

A2 is the last load-bearing spec because it depends on everything else:

· Actions have preconditions → need A5 (SPL).
· Actions modify state → need A4 (state schema).
· Actions are committed → need A1 (FSM COMMIT state).
· Actions are referenced by plans → need A3 (Step schema).

A2 is where all four meet. It is the schema that binds the core together.

1.2 The Two Obligations

A2 has two load-bearing obligations to the Verifiable Agency Theorem:

Obligation O1 (Finiteness). The action library A is finite.

Obligation O2 (Schema Totality). Every action in A has a complete schema: preconditions, effects, envelope, cost, signature.

If O1 fails, Theorem 1 (Finiteness) fails.
If O2 fails, VERIFY (A5 §4.2) is undefined for some actions.

1.3 The Design Principle

An action is atomic, deterministic, bounded, and declared. It does not compose. It does not branch. It does not improvise.

Composition is the job of plans (A3). Atomicity is the job of actions. This separation is what makes both finite.

