2. THE ACTION SCHEMA

2.1 Definition

Definition 1 (Action). An action a ∈ A is a tuple:

```
a = ⟨ id, type, preconditions, effects, envelope, cost, signature, version ⟩
```

where:

Field Type Description
id ActionID Unique identifier (ROM-burned)
type ActionType ACTUATE \| COMMUNICATE \| COMPUTE \| REQUEST_HUMAN
preconditions Finite set of SPL predicates Required state
effects Deterministic transition State change
envelope Envelope (Definition 4) Bounds on execution
cost Cost (Definition 5) Declared cycle and energy budget
signature SK-1024 signature Authorship and integrity
version SemVer Version tag for update tracking

Every field is required. Partial actions are not admitted.

2.2 Action Types

Definition 2 (ActionType). The action type declares what kind of effect the action has:

Type Meaning State Domain Modified
ACTUATE Physical action (brake, motor, switch) s_world output projection
COMMUNICATE Message to another system s_meta.messages
COMPUTE Internal state transformation s_ctx, s_mem
REQUEST_HUMAN Escalate to human operator s_meta.escalation

Rule: the type determines which subset of state the action may modify. An action cannot modify state outside its declared type's domain.

This is the isolation property of actions, mirroring the isolation property of tiles (A6).

2.3 Atomicity Rule

Rule A2-A (Atomicity). An action has exactly one effect. It does not branch, loop, or invoke other actions.

Rationale. Composition is the plan's job (A3 §5). Atomicity keeps action verification tractable: each action is a single SPL-checkable transition. If actions could compose internally, their verification cost would be unbounded.

Consequence: the effect field is a single deterministic transition, not a sequence.

---

3. PRECONDITIONS

3.1 Definition

Definition 3 (Preconditions). The preconditions of an action a are a finite set of SPL predicates:

```
preconditions(a) = { p_1, p_2, …, p_k }
```

Each p_i is an SPL predicate over S (A5 §2).

3.2 Semantics

The preconditions of a hold in state s if and only if:

```
∀ p ∈ preconditions(a) : eval(p, s) = TRUE
```

where eval is the SPL evaluation function (A5 §2.7).

3.3 Precondition Failure

If preconditions do not hold, VERIFY returns FAIL(0x-PRECONDITION-UNMET) (A5 §4.3). The action is not committed. The FSM transitions to REFUSE_ACT (A1 §7.2).

3.4 Precondition Composition

Rule: preconditions compose by conjunction. Multiple preconditions in the set are ANDed. There is no OR between preconditions at the top level.

Rationale. Disjunctive preconditions would complicate VERIFY and interact poorly with the envelope. If a deployment needs "action allowed if X or Y," it defines two actions, each with its own precondition.

This keeps the schema canonical. Two actions with the same effect but different preconditions is the right decomposition.

3.5 Bounded Size

Rule: |preconditions(a)| ≤ K_pre, a declared constant (recommended: 8).

Predicates beyond K_pre are rejected at ROM burn time.

---

4. EFFECTS

4.1 Definition

Definition 4 (Effect). The effect of an action a is a deterministic transition on state:

```
effect(a) : S → S
```

For every s ∈ S, effect(a)(s) ∈ S is the post-state.

4.2 Determinism

Rule A2-D (Effect Determinism). effect(a) is a function, not a relation. Same input state → same output state. Always.

This is the determinism obligation of A2 to the Verifiable Agency Theorem (Corollary 5, Definition 5 of the proof).

4.3 Totality

Rule A2-T (Effect Totality). effect(a) is defined for every state in S where preconditions hold. For states where preconditions do not hold, VERIFY refuses before the effect is applied — so the effect's behavior outside its precondition domain is unreachable.

4.4 Effect Representation

Effects are represented as SPL-expressible state assignments:

```
effect(a) = { s.field_1 := expr_1,
              s.field_2 := expr_2,
              … }
```

where each expr_i is a bounded expression over SPL atoms and constants.

Rule: effect expressions cannot reference other actions, cannot call functions, and cannot include unbounded quantifiers. They are the same shape as SPL atoms — comparisons, arithmetic on bounded values, and selections.

4.5 The Effect Boundedness Property

Proposition A2-E (Effect Boundedness). For every action a, the effect expression effect(a) evaluates in bounded time.

Proof. Effect expressions are compositions of SPL atoms and bounded arithmetic (Definition 4.4). By Theorem A5-D (SPL decidability), every atom evaluates in bounded time. Composition of boundedly-many bounded operations is bounded. □

4.6 Effects Cannot Modify Immutable State

Rule: effect expressions cannot write to fields marked immutable in A4 (e.g., ROM constants, plan IDs). Attempts to do so are rejected at ROM burn time.

---

5. ENVELOPE

5.1 Definition

Definition 5 (Envelope). The envelope of an action a is a finite set of SPL predicates:

```
envelope(a) = ⟨ precond_env, magnitude, rate, duration, abort ⟩
```

where:

Component Meaning
precond_env Additional preconditions for envelope safety
magnitude Bounds on action parameter magnitudes
rate Bounds on the change rate of parameters
duration Maximum duration of the action
abort Condition under which the action must abort

Every component is an SPL predicate (possibly a conjunction of predicates).

5.2 Envelope Semantics

The envelope holds in state s if and only if all components hold:

```
envelope_holds(a, s) = precond_env(s) ∧ magnitude(s) ∧ rate(s) ∧ duration(s)
```

The abort condition is evaluated during execution, not before. It is a runtime guard.

5.3 Envelope vs. Preconditions

Aspect Preconditions Envelope
When checked Before execution Before and during
Purpose Gate the action Bound the action
Evaluated over Current state Current state + action params
Failure code 0x-PRECONDITION-UNMET 0x-ENVELOPE-VIOLATED

Both are checked by VERIFY (A5 §4.2). Both must hold for PASS.

5.4 Envelope Bound

Rule: |envelope(a)| ≤ K_env, a declared constant (recommended: 16).

---

6. COST

6.1 Definition

Definition 6 (Cost). The cost of an action a is a tuple:

```
cost(a) = ⟨ c_cycles, c_energy ⟩
```

Field Meaning
c_cycles Maximum FSM cycles to commit the action
c_energy Maximum energy (µJ) consumed

Both are declared in ROM. Both are upper bounds.

6.2 Cost Enforcement

Rule: the FSM cycle counter (A1 §5) enforces c_cycles. If the action exceeds its declared cycle budget, the FSM transitions to ERROR_TIMEOUT (A1 §7.3).

6.3 Cost Interaction with T-AET

The declared cost of an action contributes to the task-level AET budget (A1 §5.2). If a plan references an action whose cost would exceed T-AET when combined with the rest of the plan, the plan is rejected at ROM burn time.

6.4 Cost Bound

Rule: c_cycles ≤ C_max_action, a declared standard constant (recommended: 400 cycles, matching the base WCET target).

Actions exceeding this bound are rejected at ROM burn time.

---

7. THE ACTION LIBRARY

7.1 Definition

Definition 7 (Action Library). The action library is the ROM-burned set:

```
A = { a_1, a_2, …, a_n }
```

with n < ∞.

7.2 Properties

Property Meaning
Finite \|A\| = n < ∞ (Obligation O1)
Immutable Action bodies are ROM-burned. Not modified at runtime.
Versioned Each action has a version. Multiple versions may coexist.
Signed Every action carries an SK-1024 signature.
Enumerated Every action is enumerated at burn time.
Typed Every action has a declared type (§2.2).

7.3 Library Size

Rule: |A| ≤ N_A, a declared standard constant (recommended: 65,536 = 2^16).

Actions beyond N_A are rejected at burn time. This bounds the enumeration of A.

7.4 Action Lookup

Actions are looked up by id:

```
lookup(id) : ActionID → A ∪ {⊥}
```

Lookup is O(1) via ROM-burned index table.

7.5 Action Set for a Plan

A plan p references a subset of A:

```
actions(p) = { a ∈ A : a.id appears in some ActionStep of p }
```

By A3 §2.3 (bounded plan length), |actions(p)| ≤ K.

