8. ACTION AUTHORING AND ROM BURN

8.1 The Authoring Workflow

Actions are authored before ROM burn. The workflow:

1. Author writes the action schema (id, type, preconditions, effects, envelope, cost).
2. Verify the schema passes burn-time checks (§8.2).
3. Sign the schema with SK-1024.
4. Burn the schema into ROM.

Actions are never authored at runtime.

8.2 Burn-Time Checks

Before burning, every action must pass:

Check Requirement
Schema completeness All fields present (§2.1)
Precondition well-formedness All predicates valid SPL (A5 §2)
Effect well-formedness All expressions bounded (Definition 4.4)
Envelope well-formedness All predicates valid SPL
Cost bound c_cycles ≤ C_max_action
Type-domain consistency Effects stay within type's state domain (§2.2)
Immutability Effects do not write immutable fields (§4.6)
Signature Valid SK-1024 signature
ID uniqueness No two actions share an ID
Cardinality \|A\| ≤ N_A after burn

Any action failing a check is rejected. The library is admitted only in full.

8.3 The Burn-Time Verifier

Rule: the burn-time verifier is a separate component from the runtime system. It runs before ROM burn. Its output is either "admit" or "reject with reason."

The burn-time verifier is itself a formal component, subject to its own verification. This is a future spec (not yet written).

