14. CONCLUSION

A2 closes the last load-bearing assumption of the Verifiable Agency Theorem. It specifies:

1. The action schema — the atomic unit of agency.
2. The action library — the finite, ROM-burned, signed set of committable actions.
3. Atomicity — actions do not compose. Plans do.
4. Determinism and boundedness — every effect is a bounded deterministic function.
5. Burn-time verification — the checks that admit or reject actions.

With A2, the closed core of the agentic architecture is complete:

Spec Role Theorem Assumption
A1 FSM Deterministic δ, bounded T-AET
A2 Actions Finite A, schema totality
A3 Plans Finite P, bounded \|p\| ≤ K
A4 State Finite S, finite Σ
A5 Predicates Decidable V, decidable preconditions

Every assumption in the Verifiable Agency Theorem is now grounded by a specific sub-spec.

The remaining specs — A6, A7, A8, A9 — extend the architecture but do not affect the theorem's core premises:

· A6 — Tile Invocation Protocol (isolation, context hash)
· A7 — Persistent Deterministic Memory (the append-only log)
· A8 — Composition & Failure Semantics (how plans sequence at runtime)
· A9 — Verification & Certification Path (how the theorem maps to norms)

The theorem is now dischargeable in principle. Machine-checking is the next step.

