APPENDIX A — Plain Language

What is an action?
One thing the agent can do. One. Not several. Not a recipe. One thing.
Like what?
Apply the brake. Send a message. Compute a value. Ask a human.
Why is it atomic?
So it can be checked. If an action had ten steps inside it, checking it would be as hard as checking a whole plan. Atomic means one check per action.
Who writes actions?
The people who deploy the agent. They write them, verify them, sign them, and burn them into ROM.
Can the agent invent new actions?
No. It can only use the ones that were burned in.
What if the agent needs an action that wasn't burned in?
Then the plan can't be written. The agent refuses with 0x-AMBIG-GOAL.
What does each action declare?
Its preconditions — when it's allowed to run. Its effects — what it changes. Its envelope — how far it can go. Its cost — how many cycles it takes.
What's the guarantee?
The action library is finite. Every action is fully declared. Every action's effect is deterministic. Nothing surprises.
What is this called?
A2 — the action library schema.

